  doi = {10.2139/ssrn.4592255},
  url = {https://papers.ssrn.com/abstract=4592255},
  urldate = {2025-02-05},
  abstract = {The recent surge in the use of computational methods and tools in the communication sciences reflects the ongoing development of this methodological approach. However, the current knowledge regarding the computational methods and tools employed in communication science is inadequate. Hence, junior communication researchers feel challenged applying computational methods and tools in their research practice. To help address this problem, we empirically observed researchers' use of computational methods and tools by analyzing the research articles published in communication science journals that employed computational methods. We systematically searched articles published in five major peer-reviewed communication journals, written in English, between 2016 and 2021 by adhering to the PRISMA-ScR protocol. As a result, we found 59 articles that employed computational methods and tools. The use of computational methods and tools significantly increased from 2016 to 2021. Empirically-focused journals have more publications utilizing computational methods than other journals. Unsupervised machine learning is the most commonly used computational method. Python, R, Linguistic Inquiry and Word Count software (LIWC) are the most frequently used computational tools. We explain the computational methods and tools used in each article and the functionality of the tools in the result. Despite the field's rapid evolution, the validity and reliability of the methods employed and developed remain uncertain. This study can guide researchers in selecting computational methods and tools for future communication research. In addition, educators can utilize the results to inform the development of digital methodology course curricula.},
  langid = {english},
  pubstate = {prepublished},
  keywords = {communication science,computational method,PRISMA,Scoping Review},
